-- Her work. (1) "A Flexible Type System for Fearless Concurrency", Milano, Turcotti, Myers, PLDI 2022
-- (doi 10.1145/3519939.3523443): threads exchange arbitrary object graphs without destructive races.
-- Regions are static groups of objects that enter and leave a thread's reservation as a unit, held
-- linearly; the heap domination invariant is tempered (an iso field may fail to dominate while it is
-- tracked, its target region known statically); five virtual transformations (focus, unfocus, explore,
-- retract, attach) change the tracking context and not the heap; `if disconnected` is a run-time
-- test of separation whose result the type system uses; send requires an empty tracking context, recv
-- gives a fresh region; progress and preservation give that a well-typed program never fails a
-- reservation check, so the checks are erased. The checker is a prover in OCaml that searches for
-- derivations and a verifier in Coq (Gallina) that checks them. (2) Swift 6 region-based isolation
-- (SE-0414, accepted with modifications February 2024): that paper's regions on Swift's actors; she led
-- the implementation effort (her page; references Doug Gregor, Apple). (3) NilAway, Uber: nilability as
-- constraints accumulated compositionally, a contradiction nilable(S) and nonnil(S) reported with the
-- flow that witnesses it; she began its design and implementation in 2020 and Uber's team carried it on
-- (blog 15 November 2023; github.com/uber-go/nilaway). (4) "Wisening Assertions: A live Bayesian
-- reasoning system for probabilistic correctness", POPL 2023 Student Research Competition: a deployment
-- keeps a live, cheap model of where bugs can still be and stops executing assertions that have become
-- redundant; the problem she names as the hard part is relating observable events of an execution
-- (branch frequencies, monitors) to the events of interest (did information flow, was an unoptimized
-- path taken), by probabilistic graphical models in preference to a trained model, with speculative
-- compilation as a second use. (5) Hardware transactional memory at MIT: Puppetmaster, a certified
-- hardware accelerator for transaction scheduling (transactions declare read and write sets; only
-- compatible ones run together; the scheduler verified in Coq by an invariant and a simulation, Perez-
